messages.content:
You will compare one known error in a data agent's work against a ranked list of items produced by a reviewer. Your job is to decide whether any item in the list identifies this known error, and if so, which one.

An item identifies the known error only if both of these hold:
- It is about the same thing: the same table, and the same column, computation, filter, or join.
- It points to the same problem: what the item says is wrong, assumed, or missing is the same as what went wrong in the known error. It does not matter whether the item is phrased as an assumption the agent made ("the agent assumes ...") or as a problem in the work ("this does not match ..."); judge by meaning.

An item does not count if it only says that something may be wrong without saying what, such as "this column may be computed incorrectly". An item about the same column but a different problem does not count.

If more than one item identifies the error, report the highest-ranked one. If you are unsure, say so rather than guessing.

Return a JSON object with:
- "verdict": "match", "no_match", or "unsure".
- "rank": the position of the matching item (1 is the top), or null.
- "quote": the exact words from the matching item that show it identifies the error, or null.
- "reason": one short sentence.

=== Known error ===
Table: asana__team_efficiency_metrics
What the agent's work assumed: medium_projects 按项目实际任务量分档统计
What was actually required or true: 这一层 project_size_category 固定为 medium

=== Reviewer's ranked list for this table ===
[
  {
    "rank": 1,
    "item": {
      "problem": "The required task-to-user member-performance aggregation is omitted and all performer distributions are hardcoded to zero.",
      "about": "requirement",
      "evidence_in_work": "0::BIGINT developing_performers ... NULL::DOUBLE avg_tasks_per_member",
      "source": "Distributions (counts across task rows ...): top/high/solid/developing/underperformers ... avg_tasks_per_member",
      "reason": "Matched users should contribute developing-performer task rows and zero portfolio averages under the documented defaults."
    }
  },
  {
    "rank": 2,
    "item": {
      "problem": "Collaboration and resource optimization scores are hardcoded to zero even for teams with members.",
      "about": "requirement",
      "evidence_in_work": "0::INTEGER collaboration_efficiency_score,0::INTEGER resource_optimization_score",
      "source": "collaboration_efficiency_score ... based on performer mix shares; resource_optimization_score ... based on shares of high_workload_members and high_risk_members",
      "reason": "Two of the four components of team efficiency are never calculated."
    }
  },
  {
    "rank": 3,
    "item": {
      "problem": "Project size distributions are inferred from task-count bands instead of using the documented medium_project default for health records.",
      "about": "requirement",
      "evidence_in_work": "COUNT(*) FILTER(WHERE h.total_tasks BETWEEN 21 AND 50) medium_projects",
      "source": "project_health_summary: ... project_size_category defaulted to 'medium_project'",
      "reason": "The team project-size distribution is calculated from an unsupported substitute definition."
    }
  },
  {
    "rank": 4,
    "item": {
      "problem": "efficiency_grade is constant rather than classified from the overall efficiency score.",
      "about": "requirement",
      "evidence_in_work": "'needs_improvement'::VARCHAR efficiency_grade",
      "source": ">=85 highly_efficient, >=70 efficient, >=55 moderately_efficient, >=40 needs_improvement, else inefficient",
      "reason": "Teams below 40 or above other thresholds receive incorrect labels."
    }
  },
  {
    "rank": 5,
    "item": {
      "problem": "Validity omits the required score and average-rate bounds and does not filter on the full is_valid_record expression.",
      "about": "requirement",
      "evidence_in_work": "WHERE t.team_id IS NOT NULL",
      "source": "overall_team_efficiency_score, avg_completion_rate, avg_quality_rate ... within [0,100]; Output: only rows with is_valid_record=true",
      "reason": "Teams with no valid project health can be emitted despite null averages that fail the required validation."
    }
  },
  {
    "rank": 6,
    "item": {
      "problem": "efficiency_rank orders by on-schedule rate rather than overall_team_efficiency_score.",
      "about": "requirement",
      "evidence_in_work": "RANK() OVER(ORDER BY COALESCE(ps.on_schedule_rate_pct,0) DESC) efficiency_rank",
      "source": "efficiency_rank by overall_team_efficiency_score descending",
      "reason": "The published rank ignores quality, collaboration, and resource efficiency."
    }
  },
  {
    "rank": 7,
    "item": {
      "problem": "Improvement recommendations and team strengths are always empty rather than conditionally generated.",
      "about": "requirement",
      "evidence_in_work": "''::VARCHAR improvement_recommendations,''::VARCHAR team_strengths",
      "source": "improvement_recommendations: phrases appended in fixed order when conditions are met; team_strengths: phrases appended in fixed order when conditions are met",
      "reason": "The prescribed explanatory outputs are not implemented, although the supplied summary leaves their exact conditions underspecified."
    }
  },
  {
    "rank": 8,
    "item": {
      "problem": "Team maturity is hardcoded to mixed_maturity for any team with members instead of using performer shares.",
      "about": "requirement",
      "evidence_in_work": "ELSE 'mixed_maturity' END team_maturity_level",
      "source": ">=60% top+high mature_team; else >=70% top+high+solid developing_team; else underperformers<=30% mixed_maturity; else needs_development",
      "reason": "The classification cannot react to the member distributions the contract requires."
    }
  },
  {
    "rank": 9,
    "item": {
      "problem": "Project summaries are split by a joined team name but subsequently joined back to teams only by team_id.",
      "about": "data",
      "evidence_in_work": "GROUP BY p.team_id,team.team_name ... ON t.team_id=ps.team_id::VARCHAR",
      "source": "grain: One validated row per (team_id, team_name)",
      "reason": "If team IDs have multiple names, summaries can cross-match and multiply output rows despite the specified pair grain."
    }
  },
  {
    "rank": 10,
    "item": {
      "problem": "Teams without completed projects can lose health contributions because the upstream health model filters null quality percentages before applying zero defaults.",
      "about": "requirement",
      "evidence_in_work": "AVG(h.completion_percentage) avg_completion_rate,AVG(h.quality_percentage) avg_quality_rate",
      "source": "project_health_indicators: percentages ... when denominator is 0, set to 0",
      "reason": "Missing upstream projects can bias team averages and exacerbate the omitted validity checks."
    }
  }
]
